-- Written by kortopi from the data directory data, against the database shop.
-- It is the difference at the moment of the plan: 1 to insert, 0 to update, 3 to delete in 2 tables.
-- Through foreign keys, the deletions also delete 3 rows and change 7 rows.
-- Through foreign keys ON DELETE NO ACTION or RESTRICT, 2 rows make the deletions fail.
-- What the deletions do through 1 foreign key is not known.
-- Run the statements in one transaction.

-- These settings make the values below mean what they meant to the plan. SET LOCAL keeps them until the end of the transaction.
SET LOCAL settings;

-- geo.regions: 1 to insert
INSERT INTO geo.regions (2, n2);

-- The deletions, in the reverse order of the foreign keys. An apply makes them only with --allow-delete,
-- so they are comments here: remove the "-- " before every line of a statement to run it.

-- geo.cities: 2 to delete
-- DELETE FROM geo.cities WHERE (10);
-- DELETE FROM geo.cities WHERE (11);

-- geo.regions: 1 to delete
-- Through foreign keys, the deletion of this row also reaches other rows:
--   2 rows of geo.cities (region) reference it through cities_region, ON DELETE NO ACTION, so the deletion fails while these rows reference it.
--   3 rows of geo.lines (region) reference it through lines_region, ON DELETE CASCADE, so the deletion deletes these rows too.
--     7 rows of geo.stops (line) reference those rows of geo.lines through stops_line, ON DELETE SET NULL, so the deletion sets line of these rows to NULL.
--   Rows of geo.stations (region) may reference it through stations_region, ON DELETE CASCADE, but the role may not read the table, so what the deletion does to them is not known.
-- DELETE FROM geo.regions WHERE (1);
